    <worksheet name='Chest Pain Analysis'>
      <layout-options>
        <title>
          <formatted-text>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='fkGroteskNeue,ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Segoe UI,Roboto,Helvetica Neue,Arial,Noto Sans,sans-serif,Apple Color Emoji,Segoe UI Emoji,Segoe UI Symbol,Noto Color Emoji,Hiragino Sans,PingFang SC,Apple SD Gothic Neo,Yu Gothic,Microsoft YaHei,Microsoft JhengHei,Meiryo' fontsize='16'>Chest Pain Type Distribution by Disease Status</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </title>
        <caption>
          <formatted-text>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='fkGroteskNeue,ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Segoe UI,Roboto,Helvetica Neue,Arial,Noto Sans,sans-serif,Apple Color Emoji,Segoe UI Emoji,Segoe UI Symbol,Noto Color Emoji,Hiragino Sans,PingFang SC,Apple SD Gothic Neo,Yu Gothic,Microsoft YaHei,Microsoft JhengHei,Meiryo' fontsize='16'>Horizontal bar chart displaying the distribution of four chest pain types and their correlation with heart disease diagnosis. The dataset categorizes chest pain as: Type 1 (Typical Angina), Type 2 (Atypical Angina), Type 3 (Non-anginal Pain), and Type 4 (Asymptomatic). Asymptomatic patients (Type 4) show highest disease rate where as  typical angina (Type 1) present in only 2.3% patients</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </caption>
      </layout-options>
